Peer review helped me understand how looking at someone else’s code can reveal errors and edge cases that may not be noticed during individual development.
I learned that when drawing a shape using its bounding box, the radius needs to be subtracted from the mouse coordinates so that the shape is positioned with its centre at the point of the click.
Handling different mouse-button actions through a common event-dispatching method makes the code more organized and separates input handling from the actual drawing operations.
I understood the importance of keeping the canvas and the stored data synchronized. Clearing the visual canvas without updating the underlying list can lead to inconsistencies between what is displayed and what the program remembers.
Using GitHub issues to track development tasks, along with Maven-based automated tests, makes it easier to monitor progress and verify that changes are working correctly.
